Yes, on most **Amazon Linux, RHEL, and CentOS** systems, the default web root for Nginx is:

```bash
/usr/share/nginx/html
```

This is where you place your `index.html` or other static website files.

### Example

```bash
cd /usr/share/nginx/html

# Create a simple webpage
echo "<h1>Hello Nginx</h1>" > index.html
```

Then start Nginx:

```bash
sudo systemctl start nginx
```

Visit:

```text
http://<EC2-Public-IP>
```

---

### Important Nginx Paths

| Purpose                  | Default Path            |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------- |
| Web root (website files) | `/usr/share/nginx/html` |
| Main configuration       | `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf` |
| Server block configs     | `/etc/nginx/conf.d/`    |
| Log files                | `/var/log/nginx/`       |

### Interview Answer

> "On Amazon Linux, the default Nginx website directory is `/usr/share/nginx/html`. We place our HTML, CSS, JavaScript, or other static files there. Nginx serves these files to users when they access the server on port 80 or 443."

